        {
          "id": "cgs2020e1-q12c",
          "type": "review",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "stem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that \\(4+7+10+\\cdots +(3n+1)=\\frac{n(3n+5)}2\\) for all integers \\(n\\ge1\\).",
          "answer": "\\(Base n=1: 4=1(8)/2. Assume the formula for n=k. Add the next term 3k+4 and simplify to (k+1)(3(k+1)+5)/2, completing induction.\\)",
          "hint": "Check n=1 first. For the induction step, add the next term in the sequence, which is 3k+4.",
          "keyPoints": [
            {
              "text": "Use mathematical induction",
              "note": "Focus on this information from the question when choosing the method and carrying out the calculation."
            }
          ],
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "Base case: substitute \\(n=1\\) into both sides.",
              "work": "\\(\\text{LHS}=4,\\qquad\\text{RHS}=\\frac{1(3(1)+5)}2=\\frac82=4\\quad\\therefore P(1)\\text{ is true}\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Induction hypothesis: assume the statement is true for an arbitrary integer \\(k\\ge1\\).",
              "work": "\\(4+7+10+\\cdots +(3k+1)=\\frac{k(3k+5)}2\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "For \\(k+1\\), append the next term to the assumed sum.",
              "work": "\\(4+7+\\cdots +(3k+1)+(3(k+1)+1)=\\frac{k(3k+5)}2+(3k+4)\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Use a common denominator and factor the numerator.",
              "work": "\\(\\frac{3k^2+5k+6k+8}{2}=\\frac{3k^2+11k+8}{2}=\\frac{(k+1)(3k+8)}2=\\frac{(k+1)(3(k+1)+5)}2\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "This is the required formula at n=k+1. Together with the base case, induction proves the result for every integer n≥1.",
              "work": "\\(P(1)\\text{ true and }P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)\\quad\therefore P(n)\\text{ true for all }n\\ge1\\)"
            }
          ],
          "timeLimit": 60,
          "xp": 1,
