          "type": "review",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "timeLimit": 60,
          "stem": "Prove using mathematical induction that \\(2^{n+2}+3^{2n+1}\\) is divisible by 7 for all positive integers \\(n\\).",
          "hint": "Write 2^(k+3) = 2·2^(k+2) and 3^(2k+3) = 9·3^(2k+1), then substitute the assumption.",
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "Base case \\(n=1\\).",
              "work": "\\(2^{3}+3^{3}=8+27=35=7\\times5\\ \\checkmark\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Assume true for \\(n=k\\).",
              "work": "\\(2^{k+2}+3^{2k+1}=7M,\\ M\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 2^{k+2}=7M-3^{2k+1}\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Write the \\(n=k+1\\) expression in terms of the \\(n=k\\) pieces.",
              "work": "\\(2^{k+3}+3^{2k+3}=2\\cdot2^{k+2}+3^2\\cdot3^{2k+1}\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Substitute the assumption.",
              "work": "\\(=2\\left(7M-3^{2k+1}\\right)+9\\cdot3^{2k+1}=14M+7\\cdot3^{2k+1}=7\\left(2M+3^{2k+1}\\right)\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "\\(2M+3^{2k+1}\\) is an integer, so the expression is divisible by 7. By induction the result holds for all positive integers \\(n\\)."
            }
          ],
          "xp": 1,
          "meta": {
            "source": "Baulkham Hills 2020 3U Trials & Solutions",
            "school": "Baulkham Hills High School",
            "year": 2020,
            "number": "13(a)"
          },
          "answer": "n = 1: 2³ + 3³ = 35 = 7 × 5. Assume \\(2^{k+2}+3^{2k+1}=7M\\). Then \\(2^{k+3}+3^{2k+3}=2\\cdot2^{k+2}+9\\cdot3^{2k+1}=2(7M-3^{2k+1})+9\\cdot3^{2k+1}=14M+7\\cdot3^{2k+1}=7(2M+3^{2k+1})\\). True by induction.",
          "manual": true,
          "keyPoints": [
            {
              "text": "Prove using mathematical induction",
              "note": "Make 2^(k+2) the subject of the assumption and substitute."
            }
          ]
        },
